        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 916,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Takes many observations of several variables - one row per moment, one column per sensor, joint or feature - and gives, for every pair of variables, how much they vary together: the covariance.\n\nA positive covariance means the two tend to rise and fall together; negative, that one rises as the other falls; near zero, that they are unrelated. On the diagonal each variable is paired with itself, which is just its variance. The size of the numbers depends on the units and the spread of the variables; np.corrcoef scales them to -1 to 1 for comparing.\n\nThe covariance matrix is the starting point of principal component analysis:\nnp.linalg.eigh of it gives the directions along which the data varies most - the dominant axis of a gesture, the main modes of a set of joints moving together.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.cov <variables: rows or columns>\nDefault: columns.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nA 2-D array of observations, with at least two of each variable. Every input is computed and sent.\n\nvariables:\nAn option, under the node. columns (the default here) takes each column as a variable and each row as one observation - the shape a rolling buffer of samples has. rows takes each row as a variable, as NumPy itself does by default.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncovariance:\nA square matrix with one row and column per variable.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nFive observations of a, b and c. b is roughly twice a, so they vary together strongly: covariance 5. c wanders on its own: its covariances with a and b (1 and 1.7) are small for their spread. The diagonal holds each variable's variance: a 2.5, b 10.2 (twice the size, four times the variance), c 3.2.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.corrcoef gives the same relations scaled to -1 to 1. np.var is one variable's variance. np.linalg.eigh of a covariance matrix gives its principal directions.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Takes many observations of several variables - one row per moment, one column per sensor, joint or feature - and gives, for every pair of variables, how much they vary together: the covariance.\n\nA positive covariance means the two tend to rise and fall together; negative, that one rises as the other falls; near zero, that they are unrelated. On the diagonal each variable is paired with itself, which is just its variance. The size of the numbers depends on the units and the spread of the variables; np.corrcoef scales them to -1 to 1 for comparing.\n\nThe covariance matrix is the starting point of principal component analysis:\nnp.linalg.eigh of it gives the directions along which the data varies most - the dominant axis of a gesture, the main modes of a set of joints moving together.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.cov <variables: rows or columns>\nDefault: columns.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nA 2-D array of observations, with at least two of each variable. Every input is computed and sent.\n\nvariables:\nAn option, under the node. columns (the default here) takes each column as a variable and each row as one observation - the shape a rolling buffer of samples has. rows takes each row as a variable, as NumPy itself does by default.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncovariance:\nA square matrix with one row and column per variable.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nFive observations of a, b and c. b is roughly twice a, so they vary together strongly: covariance 5. c wanders on its own: its covariances with a and b (1 and 1.7) are small for their spread. The diagonal holds each variable's variance: a 2.5, b 10.2 (twice the size, four times the variance), c 3.2.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.corrcoef gives the same relations scaled to -1 to 1. np.var is one variable's variance. np.linalg.eigh of a covariance matrix gives its principal directions."
        },
